---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'adoption' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Adoption: assist → warn → enforce"
  description="A safe path from assist to enforce: the rollout sequence, five prerequisites, rollback procedure, and the metrics to watch before making compliance mandatory.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / adoption</p>
          <h1>Adoption: assist → warn → enforce</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Making the convention a merge requirement is an operational decision, not just a configuration change. The safest approach is gradual: help first, measure second, enforce only when the prerequisites are in place and rollback has been tested.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The rollout sequence</h2>
          <div class="timeline">
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--fg-faint)"><span class="wk">week 0–2</span><span class="md">assist</span><span class="ds">Install the extension and let people use it. No status is published. Nothing is measured.</span></div>
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--warn)"><span class="wk">week 2–6</span><span class="md">warn</span><span class="ds">The server starts publishing a status that never fails. You can now measure compliance without changing the reviewer's day-to-day experience.</span></div>
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--block)"><span class="wk">then</span><span class="md">enforce</span><span class="ds">Start with a single pilot repository. All prerequisites should be met, and the rollback procedure should already have been rehearsed.</span></div>
            <div class="tcell" style="--st:var(--accent)"><span class="wk">after that</span><span class="md">rollout</span><span class="ds">Expand repository by repository, based on what you learned from the pilot.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Extension</th><th>Server</th><th>Effect on the pull request</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m">off</td><td class="d">Does nothing.</td><td class="d">Publishes nothing.</td><td class="d">None.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">assist</td><td class="d">Helps reviewers and reports diagnostics. Never blocks.</td><td class="d">Publishes nothing.</td><td class="d">None.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="color:var(--warn)">warn</td><td class="d">Behaves exactly like <code>assist</code>.</td><td class="d">Publishes a status that always passes while reporting non-compliance in its summary.</td><td class="d">Visible, never blocking.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="color:var(--block)">enforce</td><td class="d">Prevents non-compliant comments from being submitted.</td><td class="d">Publishes a required status that fails while an unresolved blocking thread remains.</td><td class="d">Merge blocked.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small">For reviewers, <code>assist</code> and <code>warn</code> behave exactly the same. The only difference is on the server side. That makes <code>warn</code> a genuine measurement phase: you can observe how the convention is being followed without changing anyone's workflow and distorting the results.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Prerequisites for <code style="font-size:.9em">enforce</code></h2>
          <p>Three requirements apply everywhere. Azure DevOps adds two more.</p>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Configure <code>resolverOverrideGroup</code></b><span class="tx">An authorized group must be defined before enabling <code>enforce</code>. Without one, neither resolution override is available: nobody can record a <code>decision</code>, and nobody can exempt a pull request. If the author of a blocking thread is unavailable, that thread can leave the pull request permanently blocked. Whenever an <code>enforce</code> repository resolves to an empty group, the server emits a <code>config-warning</code>.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Document the rollback procedure and assign an owner</b><span class="tx">“We'll work it out if something goes wrong” is not a rollback plan. Name the person responsible and make sure they have access to both the organization configuration channel and the server's administration endpoint.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>Make the check required and disable bypass</b><span class="tx">On GitHub, enable <em>Require status checks to pass</em> for <code>conventional-comments</code>, and also enable <em>Do not allow bypassing the above settings</em>. On Azure DevOps, configure a <em>Status checks</em> branch policy for <code>conventional-comments/compliance</code> and set it to <em>Block</em>, not <em>Warn</em>. Requiring the check without disabling bypass leaves administrators outside the guarantee.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk n">4</span><b>Azure DevOps: request exemptions through the server</b><span class="tx">Azure DevOps does not report who applied a label. To preserve an audit trail, the server applies the exemption label itself and records who requested it. There is nothing extra to configure, but exemptions must be requested through the administration endpoint rather than applied manually in the UI.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk n">5</span><b>Azure DevOps: keep detection latency at 60 seconds or less</b><span class="tx">Either provide an event channel or set <code>reconcileIntervalSeconds</code> to <code>60</code> or less. In <code>enforce</code>, the server emits a <code>config-warning</code> when the interval is greater than 60 seconds.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Rollback</h2>
          <div class="callout blk">
            <span class="ct">the rule to remember</span>
            <h4>Roll back to <code>warn</code>. Never to <code>assist</code>, and never to <code>off</code>.</h4>
            <p>In <code>warn</code>, the server keeps publishing a status, but that status always passes. That means every pull request becomes unblocked immediately, without changing branch protection. <code>assist</code> and <code>off</code> are different: the server publishes no status at all. If <code>conventional-comments</code> is still configured as a required check, switching from <code>enforce</code> to either of those modes leaves every pull request waiting for a status that will never arrive. You would then have to remove the requirement repository by repository. During an incident, switching to <code>off</code> can turn the rollback itself into a second incident.</p>
          </div>
          <ul class="tick">
            <li><span class="mk">1</span><b>Set <code>mode</code> to <code>warn</code> in the organization configuration</b><span class="tx">Moving to a less restrictive mode is applied to pull requests that are already open. You do not need to wait for them to close.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">2</span><b>Let cache invalidation propagate</b><span class="tx">The organization configuration is checked every 60 seconds independently of the normal cache. As soon as the observed mode becomes less restrictive than the previous one, the entire configuration cache is invalidated automatically. If you need the change immediately, call <code>POST /admin/cache/invalidate</code>.</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk">3</span><b>Verify the result on a real pull request</b><span class="tx">Confirm that a green <code>warn</code> status appears on a witness pull request. Check the behaviour itself, not just the configuration file.</span></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="small">The target defined by the specification is simple: rollback every repository within minutes, without touching repositories individually. If your rollback procedure requires looping over repositories, it is not sufficient.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>What <code style="font-size:.9em">warn</code> measures</h2>
          <p>There are six indicators. The first two help determine whether moving to <code>enforce</code> is reasonable. The last one helps detect whether enforcement is being routinely bypassed.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m">complianceRate</td><td class="d">Percentage of human comments with no error-severity diagnostic. This is the primary figure to examine before enabling <code>enforce</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">warningRate</td><td class="d">Percentage of human comments that produce warnings but no errors. A high but stable value may be perfectly acceptable because warnings never block.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">labelDistribution</td><td class="d">Number of comments using each label. If usage collapses mostly onto <code>note</code> and <code>nitpick</code>, reviewers may be avoiding labels that can become blocking.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">serviceAccountShare</td><td class="d">Percentage of comments written by bots or service accounts. Helps verify that <code>toolCommands</code> and <code>exemptUsers</code> are configured appropriately.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">totalHumanComments</td><td class="d">The sample size behind the percentages. A rate calculated from twelve comments tells you very little.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m" style="color:var(--warn)">decisionsInBlockingThreads</td><td class="d">How often blocking threads are resolved through an override. A steadily rising value can indicate that enforcement is being bypassed rather than followed. Check it before expanding the rollout.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Configuration for each phase</h2>
          <p>Only one setting needs to change as you move from one phase to the next: <code>mode</code>. Everything else should already be in place.</p>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-k">&#123;</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"version"</span>: <span class="tk-v">1</span>,
  <span class="tk-k">"mode"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"warn"</span>,                    <span class="tk-cm">// assist → warn → enforce</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"resolverOverrideGroup"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"acme/tech-leads"</span>],
  <span class="tk-k">"formatSeverity"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"warn"</span>,          <span class="tk-cm">// formatting issues do not block</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"exemptUsers"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"dependabot[bot]"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"github-actions[bot]"</span>],
  <span class="tk-k">"toolCommands"</span>: [<span class="tk-v">"/*"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"@dependabot"</span>, <span class="tk-v">"@renovate"</span>],
  <span class="tk-k">"server"</span>: <span class="tk-k">&#123;</span>
    <span class="tk-k">"gracePeriodSeconds"</span>: <span class="tk-v">900</span>,
    <span class="tk-k">"reconcileIntervalSeconds"</span>: <span class="tk-v">60</span>
  <span class="tk-k">&#125;</span>
<span class="tk-k">&#125;</span></pre>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">why 60 seconds</span>
            <p style="margin:0"><code>reconcileIntervalSeconds</code> is set to <strong>60 here, not 900</strong>. On Azure DevOps, prerequisite 5 requires a value of 60 seconds or less when using <code>enforce</code>. Since moving between phases should only require changing <code>mode</code>, this value must already be correct before enforcement is enabled. On GitHub, thread state is covered by an event channel, so a value of 900 is acceptable.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="ct">upgrade note</span>
            <p style="margin:0"><code>toolCommands</code> is <strong>empty by default</strong>. Slash commands such as <code>/rebase</code> used to be recognized through a built-in exemption list. That list no longer exists. In <code>enforce</code>, a bare <code>/rebase</code> is therefore rejected like any other non-compliant comment unless it has been declared. Adding <code>"/*"</code> exempts all slash commands.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="pager">
          <a class="pgl" href={href('labels', 'en')}><span class="dir">← previous</span><span class="ttl">Labels &amp; decorations</span></a>
          <a class="pgl" href={href('server', 'en')} style="text-align:right"><span class="dir">next →</span><span class="ttl">Server companion</span></a>
        </div>
</Docs>
